// Vermont income tax withholding: GB-1210-2026, "2026 Income Tax
// Withholding Instructions, Tables, and Charts", Vermont Percentage Method
// Withholding Tables (wages paid in 2026):
//   1. one allowance (from the table's footnote, e.g. $103.85 weekly) x
//      allowances claimed on Form W-4VT
//   2. subtract from wages
//   3. the period's single or married table (3.35% / 6.60% / 7.60% / 8.75%)
// Tables generated by tools/state/generate_vt.py from GB-1210. The guide's
// example ($1,800 weekly, married, 2 allowances -> $45.77) is in the tests.
// Civil union partners use the married table.
//
// W-4VT input: { filingStatus: "single" | "married" |
//   "marriedWithholdAtSingle", allowances, additionalWithholdingCents?,
//   exempt? }.
// No W-4VT: "An employer may use the information from federal form W-4 if a
// Vermont form is not submitted" - a pre-2020 W-4's marital status and
// allowances, or a 2020+ W-4's filing status (joint -> married) with no
// allowances; and "If the federal W-4 indicates an additional amount of
// federal withholding ... the Vermont withholding should be increased by
// 30% of the extra federal withholding." With no form at all: single, no
// allowances (the guide is silent; flagged).
//
// Wages: wages as defined under IRC 3401 (GB-1210), so pre-tax 401(k) is
// excluded.
//
// Not covered: the employee share of Vermont's Child Care Contribution (the
// 0.44% payroll tax; employers may withhold up to 0.11% from wages).

